  "body": "**Mathematical audit / research note; no new kappa.** Checking the sharper earlier-source guard only before the first call can miss dirty-field corruption in the later-source routine. For `n=2,K=6,G=2,rho=1`, the unchanged physical word takes `(x,y,t,u,b)=(2,124,1,0,7)` to `(2,126,5,0,7)`: the target is right, but the temporary should remain 1.\n\nThis adds a proved two-pass invariant sufficient guard and its exact correlated density. With `B=2^G`, `A=2^(K-1)`, source bits `c_i`, control parities `p_i=u_i mod 2`, and target segments `2g_i+a_i`, require:\n\n```text\nt_i <= B-2                         for i < n\nu_i + c_i < B                     for i < n-1\ng_i + (p_i OR c_i)*t_i < A         for i < n-1\n```\n\nThe complete-rectangle good fraction is `((B-1)/B)^n * (1 - 1/(2B) - (3B-2)*(B-2)/(8*A*B))^(n-1)`. At the example geometry it is **1971/4096**, versus **81/1024** for the retained later-source guard and **63/512** for PR763's source-dependent carry condition with the original target guard. All twelve swaps, ten rotations, source access, highest-bit completion and full exceptional-repair costs remain.\n\nThe original construction is correct. PR1219 explicitly excludes later-source calls, and PR763 retains the old target guard. This contribution composes their results and adds the correlated density and full-map controls. The guard describes individually safe subcalls with no interior source-load carry; it is **not the global exact agreement set**. No production predicate, supplier, selected frontier or `upstream/` file changes.\n\nReproduce with Python 3.10+ and the standard library:\n\n```sh\npython3 -I -O -B research/compact-late-guard-audit-seed73/test_package.py\n```\n\nExpected markers: `PASS_COMPACT_LATE_TWO_PASS_GUARD states=723520 admitted=293408 physical=1728 no_new_kappa`, then `PASS_COMPACT_LATE_GUARD_PACKAGE ... faults=7`.\n\nValidation passes on macOS arm64 with CPython **3.14.6 and 3.12.13**, including the registered package runner, minimal read-only relocation, all six complete quotient domains, full-field physical comparisons, exact density counts, 24,576 repair payloads, eight named mathematical controls and seven subprocess faults. The wrong repair key fills all 17,376 holes but misplaces their payloads. All **17 existing compact-control tests**, selected-record integrity, metadata/source pins and staged whitespace checks pass. The clean patch applies to refreshed main. Details and unrun checks are in `VALIDATION.json`.\n\nBase: **49b3157d1d89adcef16df8be98445451672be5ba**. Seed **73**, adversarial audit. Refreshed main **dd6403f774a0891093a006ae9716c02e371b1fdb** leaves all six consumed inputs and contribution guidance unchanged. Exact source pins and predecessor documents are retained. The complete final text-search snapshot covers 843 open PRs and 16 Discussion records; this is bounded overlap evidence, not a novelty certificate. No pinned sprint issue exists, so the authorized local claim/result is in `CLAIM.json`.\n\nThe all-n statement rests on the written integer proof. Finite replay is supporting evidence; physical/compiler, recursive field-reservation, hosting, precision and analytic obligations remain. Full supplier cold replay, the full repository suite, Linux, hosted CI success and proof-assistant verification are not claimed.\n\nCredit Douglas Colkitt and OpenAI's original framework; John-Michael Smith / @jp3isme's #763 (`a5d3436b82f5a58033ec6b490ebb23db0c1860c6`); Hanson Sun / @bbMisk's #1219 (`dfb453c88b03868d346014867a091d36e7b16589`); and @martinxxx9's #1108. No predecessor audit implementation is imported. Prepared for **Hanson Sun (@bbMisk)** with substantial **OpenAI Codex** assistance in mathematics, implementation, verification and writing. Apache-2.0; no independent human review or literature-priority claim.\n\nThe README and TASK metadata include the scoped open question about adoption at a concrete recursive caller and its physical-contract blockers.\n",
